<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivrea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eporedia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivrea is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dora-baltea">Dora Baltea</a> where it leaves the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 100 BCE as the colony of Eporedia to guard the Alpine routes, it was later the seat of the March of Ivrea, whose rulers Berengar II and Arduin became kings of Italy. In the 20th century it was the home of the Olivetti company, and its industrial town is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivrea.jpg" alt="Ivrea" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Valerio Bozzolan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linux_Day_Ivrea_2021_-_visione_d%27insieme_da_Piazza_Rondolino_direzione_Ovest_naviglio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ivrea stands at about 267 metres above sea level on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dora-baltea">Dora Baltea</a>, a tributary of the Po, not far from the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta-valley">Aosta Valley</a>, on a road that formed part of the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-francigena">Via Francigena</a>. It is regarded as the capital of the Canavese area. The surrounding country was shaped by a large Pleistocene glacier, which left moraine hills known as the Ivrea Morainic Amphitheatre and, after the last glaciation retreated about 9700 BCE, a ring of lakes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name Eporedia is probably Celtic, perhaps from the Gaulish epo, horse, and reda, a four-wheeled cart.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Roman colony, the main settlement of the Salassi in the area was at Brich Appareglio, about 3 km south of the old centre, occupied from the Final Bronze Age until the fifth century BCE and briefly again in the later second century BCE. The colony of Eporedia was founded in 100 BCE, in the sixth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a>, as a colony of Roman citizens on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dora-baltea">Dora Baltea</a>. It was meant to control the Salassi, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> finally subjugated in 25 BCE. Remains include an amphitheatre and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> period Ivrea was the seat of a duchy. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in the 9th century it was a county capital. In 1001, after disputes with Bishop Warmund, who ruled the city, Arduin conquered the March of Ivrea, later became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and began a dynasty that lasted until the 11th century, when the city fell again under the bishops. Ivrea became a free comune in the 12th century, fell to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> early in the 13th and was then disputed between the bishops, the marquisate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Monferrato</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-vi-count-of-savoy">Amadeus VI of Savoy</a> acquired Ivrea in 1356. Apart from a brief French conquest at the end of the 16th century it remained under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> until 1800, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> entered the city on 26 May; his control ended in 1814. In the 20th century it was the base of Olivetti, a maker of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typewriter">typewriters</a>, mechanical calculators and later computers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ivrea had 22,604 inhabitants in 2021. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed the Olivetti site, Ivrea, Industrial City of the 20th Century, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> on 1 July 2018, saying that it expresses a modern vision of the relationship between industrial production and architecture. The Ivrea Carnival is known for the Battle of the Oranges, thrown over three days by townspeople divided into teams at carriages that represent a duke's guard.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivrea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At an assembly of Lombard nobles at Pavia, Arduin, margrave of <b>Ivrea</b>, was crowned King of Italy after the death of Emperor Otto III.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-15">15 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The forces of Rudolph II of Burgundy and Adalbert I of <b>Ivrea</b> defeated Berengar I of Italy at Fiorenzuola, leaving Berengar de facto dethroned and Rudolph King of Italy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-29">29 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late 10th century the county was held by a branch of the house of Burgundy-<b>Ivrea</b> descended from Otto-William, who also ruled the Free County of Burgundy.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-macon">County of Mâcon</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 888–891 Guy of Spoleto, contending for the Italian crown, created a march around <b>Ivrea</b> for his Burgundian ally Anscar, guarding the Alpine passes of the Aosta valley and the western Po plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-ivrea">March of Ivrea</a></li><li><blockquote>The Salassi lived in the Aosta valley and around <b>Eporedia</b> (Ivrea), at the foot of the passes over the western Alps.</blockquote><a href="/state/salassi">Salassi</a></li></ul></section>
